<table>
<tr><td>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=../Graphics/Australia_20050921.jpg"><img class="PicBorder" src="Graphics/AustraliaSmall_20050921.jpg" align="left"></a>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=../Graphics/Australia_20050921.jpg"><img class="PicBorder" src="Graphics/AustraliaSmall_20050921.jpg" align="right"></a>
<u>01-SEP-2005 10:28 PM (AUS Time)</u><br>
Little did I know, today would turn out to be a disastrous and costly day. I knew that I would have a taste of the good and the bad on the trip but today I got a taste of the bad.  M and I wasted the morning away trying to get a car hire (here car rentals are called car hires).  We had not gotten our bearings down in Cairns yet and therefore could not find any of the car rental places we had noted the previous night.   M was tired and I had her sit down as I did some fast walking and found a few shops.  I finally got a car negotiated to a price I liked. $39 AUD/Day.  This was for a dinky little economy, 2 door, manual hatchback.  In the US, it would have been a lot cheaper.  Especially since I wasn't getting it from the airport.  Anyway, I needed the car so I went to reach in my wallet for my credit card. They have this thing called an excess. They will charge your card upfront for a large damage deposit, and if you return the car in one piece, you get it all back.  It's known as an <i>excess</i>.  Anyway, I realized I left my card back at the hostel and it was too far away for me to go and get it.  I had to run back over to M and get her to come with me and give her credit card and sign for it.  She had a platinum card and as I recall, they have insurance coverage on it.  Luckily, they were only 3 blocks away so M didn't have to go far.  However, when I got back to the car hire place, called A1 car rentals, they gave me a different car than they had originally promised. I had argued a little bit about it but realized that the car they were giving me was more fuel economical, so I took it.
<p>
This is the first time ever for me to drive in a foreign, in a car were the steering wheel is on the right side, and using a manual with my left hand.  Wouldn't you know it, the first thing I do is I accidentally honk the horn trying to get use to the car.  Then I couldn't get it into reverse correctly so I would lunge forward.  But after a minute or two, I got the hang of it and got out of there.
<p>
Driving in a foreign country is even crazier than walking.  Here in Australia, they have an ingenious thing called a roundabout.  I don't know why we don't have them in the US. They eliminate the need for stop lights and don't slow up traffic much.  The only thing is getting use to how one works.  But after monitoring one for a minute, I figured it out...or so I thought.  I still hadn't figured out the road signs completely and that damn near got me in a wreck.
<p>
The other annoyance of driving with the steering wheel on the right is that the windshield wiper wand and the turn signal wand are switched. I still cannot get use to it.  Everytime I want to take a turn, I turn on the windshield wipers.  Aaack!
<p>
It was almost noon now but, it was off to our first destination -- the Atherton Tablelands.  Taking a mountainous windy road we slowly got there, however today, the weather was not our friend.  It had been cloudy and raining and very poor visibility a lot of the time.  And the windy roads on the mountain had no guardrails.  Plus the car hire agent told me that car will slip on wet pavement.  I was a little scared about this, so I took my time.  
<p>
From the night before, I had realized the sun sets around late 5 PM early 6 PM.  It took 2 hrs just to drive there, giving us only about 3 hrs of light to really enjoy everything.  And the Tablelands had much to offer.  But the combination of rain, low visibility, and short on time, we only got to see about half of the things.  Also, the map we had was not correct and not to scale so we managed to get lost a lot.  However, we did finally get to Atherton.  And the town itself was small but along the way, we stopped at several places.  Namely the Curtain Fig Tree.  Quite a vicious tree, not to humans, but to other trees.  
<p>
Fig trees in general are strangler trees. They find other trees, attached themselves to these other trees, grow outward and downward from them, providing long roots, and eventually take over the tree causing it to decay away.  Then what's left is just the fig tree high in the sky with long roots.  Quite a nasty bugger. But common in the rainforests of Northern Queensland.
<p>
We had passed up two lakes which would have been great to see had it not been raining and foggy.  The rain reminded me of the the slow Portland drizzle.  The next stop was the Crystal Caves.  We had expected something cool, but were treated to some manmade tourist trap which we quickly left once we figured out.  We decided to take a different route to see some waterfalls and a crater.  We came across an overlook which showed the rolling hills and valleys against the mountains.  Volcanoes had caused cinder cones to be formed across the land.  
<p>
The next stop on our way to the waterfalls was called Hastings Swamp, which is a great place for bird watching. With over 200 species of birds spotted at this swamp, it was a bird lover's paradise.  But today, because of the rain, we only spotted a few and we had to make a dash for it in the hard pounding rain.  Yes, it turned from drizzle to hard rain.  
<p>
Going onto the next spot called Dinner Falls (and our first falls in Australia) and the Crater, we got out and walked to a typical waterfall.  Maybe if I wasn't use to the waterfalls in Oregon and Washington, I would have been more impressed but I was not. There wasn't anything special about it.  In great dismay, M and I walked over to the Crater.  And finally, something to make my jaw drop.  We were on a high overlook and over the edge a long ways down was this huge hole filled with a water and coated with bright green algae.  I hadn't seen anything like it before.  The description on the board next to the overlook read that the crater was formed from a volcanic eruption where only gas escaped from under the ground, creating the crater.  The crater made me feel like the trip was worth it.  Another visitor, who was from Australia, was talking to us at the crater. He had suggested going to see the Little Mainstream and Mainstream Falls near Ravenshoe.  He specifically kept mentionally that Mainstream Falls looked like a little version of Niagara.  So we had to check it out.
<p>
<!--
<img src=http://www.mybigadventure.com/ankesh/picupload/Uploads/Images/1126437628_1.jpg>-->
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=../picupload/Uploads/Images/1126437628_1.jpg"><img src="picupload/Uploads/Images/Small/1126437628_1.jpg"></a>
<p>
It was now starting to get dark so we hurried over to what would be the last two falls of the day.  But of course we had gotten lost.  The maps we had was pathetic and the road signs weren't helpful. We stopped off and asked for help and found the Little Mainstream Falls. It was a pretty cool looking waterfall. A couple of streams of water combined into one and formed it.
<p>
The sun was just about set when we made an attempt for Mainstream Falls.  But of course, we got lost again but we figured it out.  And then I had my first encounter spotting a kangaroo out in the wild.  It was crouched down low and just staring at me as I drove by.
<p>
We had finally gotten to Mainstream Falls. Nobody was out there.  All along in the forests. Kinda creepy.  We checked it out quickly as the sun had set and darkness was coming quickly.  It did actually remind me of a little version of Niagara.  On the way out, a small roo hopped acrossed the road and into the bush, making it my 2nd encounter with a roo.
<p>
Now the bad part about being in a foreign country where you don't know the road signs, the roads are poorly lit, you aren't use to driving in a car with a right side steering wheel is the feeling of getting lost with noone to help you.  I was hurrying to get home as I was a little panicked about being out in the middle of nowhere.  And in my hurry, I passed up a cop, specifically a photo radar cop that took my picture.  I had been speeding and didn't realize it.  Great, just what I wanted....   But I bit my lip slowed down to a speed that seemed reasonably slow as I wasn't sure how to tell the speed limits on the sign and just wanted to get home to end my bad day.  So now I expect to get some hefty fine in the mail because the rental car company has my home address and the cops will surely track me down.  But oh well, it was too late, and I wouldn't let it ruin the rest of the trip. The next morning, I had figured out what the speed limit signs looked like.  One thing I wish I would have done is study up on the driving rules in the foreign countries I was going to drive in.  It would be extra nice if the rental car agencies could give out some simple pamphlets to international drivers. It really would help.  Oh well, live and learn I say.  I'll deal with this when I get back home, unless they decide to forget about it.
<p>
